Deep learning achieves strong image-classification performance but at substantial training cost. This paper adapts ROCKET, originally a time-series method, to two-dimensional image data: large banks of random 2D convolutional kernels are summarized with simple aggregation statistics and fed to a linear classifier, with no learned filters and none of a deep CNN's hierarchical, multi-scale composition. On MNIST and CIFAR-10, used as controlled benchmarks rather than target applications, a final configuration (5,000 mixed-scale kernels) reaches 99.41%/73.43% accuracy, slightly ahead of a shallow two-layer CNN and decisively ahead of a linear SVM -- not evidence of competing with genuinely deep networks, which are out of scope. The intended niche, texture-like imagery, is confirmed on KTH-TIPS (94.7% vs. 43.5%/52.4% for SVM/CNN) and tested directly, with ten-seed paired significance testing against both baselines, on three real-world datasets: 2D-ROCKET wins significantly on texture-dominated surface-defect (NEU) and texture-attribute (DTD) data, and loses significantly, more narrowly, on non-texture chest X-ray pneumonia screening, where the pathology is spatially localized rather than textural. Adding a previously unimplemented positional-pooling feature (LSPV) closes that gap to statistical indistinguishability, confirmed with a capacity-matched control. A further test on genuine 1D time series -- raw Sentinel-1 SAR echoes, for radio-frequency-interference detection -- extends this texture-vs-localized-anomaly account to a non-image domain, again beating a linear SVM but not a CNN. 2D-ROCKET emerges as a lightweight, interpretable baseline for texture-dominated and low-data regimes -- with a deployed model 3--72x smaller than the CNN baselines across all tasks -- not a general competitor to deep CNNs.
